R7 green. Now test 2: R8 — quantidade de encontros. Add to the spec file.

R8: palestra exatamente 1 encontro; minicurso 2–5. Violation → 422 QUANTIDADE_DE_ENCONTROS.

I need to send bodies that would otherwise be valid. For palestra: tipo 'palestra', 2 encontros → 422. For minicurso with 1 encontro → 422; 6 encontros → 422. minicurso 2 → 201; 5 → 201.

Note: for palestra with 2 encontros, I need valid encontros (1h duration, within window). My corpoValido has 2 encontros, 1h each, in window. For palestra test, change tipo to 'palestra' and keep 2 encontros → QUANTIDADE_DE_ENCONTROS. For minicurso 1 encontro → remove one. For 6 encontros → add more (need valid ones; 6 encontros... but wait, if I add a 3rd, I must keep validity—they can't overlap! For minicurso with 5 or 6 encontros, the encontros must not overlap with each other and be within window. Let me build encontros across the 19-23/10 window.

Event days: 19-23 Oct 2026. Encontros at 19:00-20:00 each day. That gives up to 5 distinct non-overlapping times: 19/10, 20/10, 21/10, 22/10, 23/10. So a minicurso with 5 encontros = one per day. 6 encontros → need a 6th valid encontro that doesn't overlap... There's only 5 days in the window. But 6th could be on another day of same tipo? The window is 19-23/10; anywhere outside is invalid. So a 6 encontro minicurso within the window without overlaps is impossible! 

Hmm. But wait — that's the point of a test for R8: minicurso com 6 encontros → 422 QUANTIDADE_DE_ENCONTROS. The encontros themselves could be valid if they fit. But we can't fit 6 valid non-overlapping 1h encontros in 5 days... unless the first five are 19-23/10 and a sixth... there is no sixth. So can the 6-encontro test even reach a state where ENCONTRO_INVALIDO wouldn't trigger first? The R12 order is QUANTIDADE_DE_ENCONTROS first, so regardless, 6 encontros → 422 QUANTIDADE_DE_ENCONTROS. The test just needs 6 encontros (with whatever timestamps); the quantity rule fires first per R12. Even if encontros would be invalid, quantity comes first. So I can make the 6 encontros "valid-ish" (1h each within window, but overlapping is unavoidable—doesn't matter because quantity fires first). But to be honest, I should test the quantity rule in isolation: build 6 encontros, quantity check returns 422 QUANTIDADE_DE_ENCONTROS. Per R12, quantity is first, so overlap won't matter. But for the "5 encontros → 201" case, I need 5 valid non-overlapping encontros — one per day 19-23/10.

If I use a different type of quantity violation to also check, the criterion 6 says: palestra com 2 → 422; minicurso com 1 → 422; minicurso com 6 → 422; minicurso 2 → 201; minicurso 5 → 201.

For 6 encontros, I'll just put 6 encontros (one per day + a 6th that overlaps, e.g., another 19/10 19:00-20:00 — the quantity error fires first). Actually simpler: to keep it clean, use 6 encontros each 1h within days... there are only 5 days, so a 6th cannot be valid. I'll just add a 6th encontro duplicating a time. The test asserts QUANTIDADE_DE_ENCONTROS, which fires first per R12, so fine.

Actually, to be safe conceptually, my implementation must check quantity BEFORE encontro validity (R12). So test for 6 will get QUANTIDADE_DE_ENCONTROS regardless of encontro validity. Good.

Let me write a helper for encontros: `encontros(n)` generating one per day starting 19/10:
```js
function encontros(quantidade) {
  const dias = [];
  const diaBase = new Date('2026-10-19T19:00:00-03:00');
  for (let i = 0; i < quantidade; i++) {
    const inicio = new Date(diaBase.getTime() + i * 24 * 3600 * 1000);
    const fim = new Date(inicio.getTime() + 60 * 60 * 1000);
    dias.push({ inicio: inicio.toISOString(), fim: fim.toISOString() });
  }
  return dias;
}
```
Wait but toISOString gives UTC (Z) — that's fine, the API compares instants. But careful: adding i*24h from 19/10 gives 20,21,22,23... For 5 encontros: 19,20,21,22,23. For 6: 24/10 — outside window. So for the 6-encontro test, the 6th encontro is on 24/10 → would be ENCONTRO_INVALIDO if visited — but quantity fires first. Fine.

Actually to produce a proper 6th that at least could be argued... it doesn't matter. Quantity first.

Actually, let me reconsider using explicit ISO strings rather than computed, to keep tests readable and deterministic with Brasília offsets. The days are 19–23/10/2026, all in -03:00 (no DST in Oct in Brasília; DST ended historically... Brazil abolished DST in 2019. So -03:00 constant). I'll write encontros with explicit times.

Let me define in the spec:
```js
function encontrosDaSemana(quantidade) {
  const dia = (d) => `2026-10-${d}T19:00:00-03:00`;
  const pares = [
    { inicio: dia(19), fim: dia(19).replace('19:00', '20:00') },
    { inicio: dia(20), fim: dia(20).replace('19:00', '20:00') },
    { inicio: dia(21), fim: dia(21).replace('19:00', '20:00') },
    { inicio: dia(22), fim: dia(22).replace('19:00', '20:00') },
    { inicio: dia(23), fim: dia(23).replace('19:00', '20:00') },
  ];
  return pares.slice(0, quantidade);
}
```

That gives valid non-overlapping 1h encontros, one per day.

For the 6-encontro case, I'll append a 6th that duplicates the first (overlap) — quantity check fires first regardless:
```js
const seisEncontros = [...encontrosDaSemana(5), { inicio: dia... }];
```
Simpler: `encontrosDaSemana(5).concat(...encontrosDaSemana(1))` — 6 encontros.

